// @emit-error Call sites pin `add` at (i32, i32), (f64, f64)
// A generic (un-annotated-param) function passed to a higher-order function whose CALLBACK
// PARAMETER is itself un-annotated has no declared type to materialize against, so it reaches the
// emitter's value floor. `monoCoerceFnValue` pins a generic argument from the callee's WRITTEN
// parameter annotation; `apply(g, x, y)` writes none, so there is nothing to pin from — the
// remaining gap in the value-position family, and the one that needs the callback's type inferred
// from the HOF's own BODY rather than from an annotation. Annotating `g: (i32, i32) => i32` is the
// working spelling (see `generic-fn-param-return-boundary.vl`).
//
// What this case pins is the DIAGNOSTIC's informative branch. `add` IS monomorphized here — twice,
// at (i32, i32) and (f64, f64) — so the reject can name the concrete signatures the program's own
// call sites already prove it wants, which is exactly the annotation to write. The message also
// names the function and which of its parameters are the holes. The no-call-site branch, where
// there is no inferred signature to offer, is `error-generic-alias-never-called.vl`.
function add(a, b) { return a + b }
function apply(g, x, y) { return g(x, y) }

print(add(1, 2))
print(add(1.5, 2.5))
print(apply(add, 1, 2))
